WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.664
In this video, you'll tour the Labeling tab — the address on your labels, the packaging, who pays FedEx, and the label's size and format.

00:00:09.114 --> 00:00:16.650
Three things live here: the street lines of your store address, the packaging FedEx should assume, and the printed label's format.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:22.908
From your WordPress dashboard, go to FedEx Shipping, then Settings, and click the Labeling tab.

00:00:23.358 --> 00:00:30.414
Street Lines completes the shipper address — city, state, postal code, and country come from the Account tab.

00:00:30.864 --> 00:00:38.712
Packaging Type is the packaging FedEx assumes, on labels and on every price quote — so pick the one you actually ship with.

00:00:39.162 --> 00:00:46.770
Careful: there are two Pickup Type settings. This one is written onto your labels; the one on Shipping affects price quotes.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:56.196
Payment Type sets who pays FedEx: the sender, the recipient, a third party, or collect. Return labels always bill your own account.

00:00:56.646 --> 00:01:04.086
In Label Specification, Paper 4 by 6 fits most thermal printers, and Paper Letter prints on standard office paper.

00:01:04.536 --> 00:01:14.424
Z P L 2 or E P L 2 suits thermal printers, P D F suits office printers. Keep Common 2D unless another system renders labels.

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:23.370
Nothing was saved in this tour. In Instant mode, your labels appear under Shipments and Printings — next, match products to boxes.

